<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  prudent-server :: Prudent's Quarkus backend.

  IN THIS PHASE IT COMPILES AND NOTHING ELSE. There are no resources, no entities, no datasource
  and no migrations — those arrive in Phase 2, where there is something for them to do. What this
  module exists to prove today is that the dependency seam on the jZen framework resolves, which is
  the one thing that has to be true before anything else can be built on it.
-->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <!--
    ============================================================================================
    THE EMPTY <relativePath/> IS THE DECISION, NOT A DETAIL. READ THIS BEFORE CHANGING IT.
    ============================================================================================

    An empty <relativePath/> tells Maven: do NOT look for this parent on the filesystem, resolve it
    from a repository like any other artifact. jZen's artifacts are unpublished
    (0.1.0-SNAPSHOT, no registry), so "a repository" means the LOCAL one, ~/.m2.

    THE PREREQUISITE MAVEN CANNOT EXPRESS. Something has to put the framework artifacts in the
    local repository first, and no POM can say so — a build cannot declare "go build another
    repository". That step is:

        task zen:framework:install

    which runs `./mvnw -B install -DskipTests` inside ../jZen/server.

    WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE WHEN YOU FORGET. Maven fails with

        Non-resolvable parent POM for prudent:prudent-server:0.1.0-SNAPSHOT:
        zen:zen-parent:pom:0.1.0-SNAPSHOT was not found

    which reads like a broken or malformed POM file. It is not. The POM is fine and the fix is to
    run `task zen:framework:install`. Run `task zen:info` first if that fails too — it reports
    whether the ../jZen checkout is even there.

    WHY NOT jZen ADR-026'S FILESYSTEM <relativePath>. jZen's own reference backend inherits this
    same parent with <relativePath>../../../server/pom.xml</relativePath>, and ADR-026 has a second
    product do the equivalent across repositories, under a shared parent folder with a root
    aggregator POM above both checkouts. Prudent does not, for two reasons:

      1. CLAUDE.md requires that all work happen inside this repository and that nothing reach
         outside the repo root. A root aggregator POM in the parent directory is a file above the
         repository root, created and maintained by neither repository.
      2. A cross-repository <relativePath> couples this build to a DIRECTORY LAYOUT rather than to
         a VERSION. The empty form couples it to a coordinate, which is what the eventual published
         dependency will also be — so publishing changes this block's <version> and deletes nothing
         else.

    This is a Prudent-owned concern, so Prudent's rule wins and the divergence is recorded:
    docs/DECISIONS.md ADR-001.

    THE COST, PLAINLY: there is no version boundary between the two repositories. They move in
    lockstep, and a `git pull` in ../jZen can break this build with no version to point at.
  -->
  <parent>
    <groupId>zen</groupId>
    <artifactId>zen-parent</artifactId>
    <version>0.1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <relativePath/>
  </parent>

  <groupId>prudent</groupId>
  <artifactId>prudent-server</artifactId>
  <!--
    Declared, not inherited. It happens to match zen-parent's version string today, and inheriting
    it would silently make Prudent's version track the framework's — so that the day jZen releases
    0.2.0, Prudent's server is renumbered by a dependency. Prudent versions itself.
  -->
  <version>0.1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
  <name>Prudent :: server</name>
  <packaging>quarkus</packaging>

  <dependencies>
    <!--
      The framework libraries. No <version> on any of them: zen-parent's dependencyManagement
      supplies it, which is half of what inheriting that parent buys (the other half is the Quarkus
      BOM, the Java release level, and plugin management).

      These three and no more. zen-identity, zen-email, zen-jobs and zen-ratelimit are real
      dependencies of the finished product and are deliberately absent here — a module that
      declares a dependency it does not use implies a surface that does not exist. They arrive in
      Phase 2 alongside the auth and persistence work that needs them.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>zen</groupId>
      <artifactId>zen-core</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>zen</groupId>
      <artifactId>zen-proto</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>zen</groupId>
      <artifactId>zen-transport</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
      <artifactId>quarkus-arc</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
      <artifactId>quarkus-rest</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      NO quarkus-rest-jackson. NOT AT ANY PRIORITY. NOT LATER.

      jZen's JSON is canonical proto3 JSON produced by protobuf's JsonFormat, written by
      zen-transport's own MessageBodyWriter. quarkus-rest-jackson registers itself for
      application/json through a BUILD-TIME path that does not consult writer priority, so merely
      out-ranking it does not work: it wins, tries to reflect over a protobuf-generated class, and
      returns 500 on every proto response body. The extension must be ABSENT.

      This is on the list of rules that fail late and confusingly rather than at the point of the
      mistake, which is why it is written here rather than assumed.
    -->
  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <plugins>
      <!--
        Declared with NO <executions>, deliberately, and this module is <packaging>quarkus</packaging>
        while zen-parent registers the plugin with <extensions>true</extensions> — so that packaging's
        own lifecycle already binds generate-code, generate-code-tests and build to their phases.

        The Quarkus archetype's explicit <execution> listing those three goals is correct for a
        `jar` module and a DUPLICATE here: it binds `build` a second time, so a native build
        compiles the image TWICE and the second overwrites the first. Both builds succeed and the
        output is identical, so the only symptom is minutes of wall clock on every deploy.
      -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>${quarkus.platform.group-id}</groupId>
        <artifactId>quarkus-maven-plugin</artifactId>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>

  <profiles>
    <!--
      OpenAPI is BUILD TOOLING, and the profile is what makes that true rather than merely said.
      Prudent has no resources yet, so the extension documents nothing today; the profile is here
      now so that Phase 2's first resource lands in a module already shaped correctly, rather than
      shipping the extension to production once and being retrofitted.

      quarkus-smallrye-openapi exists to produce one file — the merged openapi.json the contract
      loop consumes. Shipping it additionally serves /openapi to the internet for the life of every
      deployed revision: a complete, current map of every route, verb, parameter and status code
      the service accepts, published to anyone who asks. Nothing needs it there.

      WHY A PROFILE AND NOT quarkus.smallrye-openapi.enable=false. That stops the endpoint and
      still bakes the extension and its scanner into the image, so the attack-surface argument
      survives and the cold-start one is untouched. Removing the dependency removes the code.

      WHY THE ACTIVATION IS INVERTED. It is ON unless -Dnative is passed, so every ordinary build,
      test run and contract regeneration has the extension and only the native production image
      lacks it. Getting this backwards is expensive and quiet: the contract task would package
      cleanly, write no openapi.json, and the downstream generator would then SKIP on its
      "does openapi.json exist?" guard — leaving generated types stale while the gate reported
      success.
    -->
    <profile>
      <id>openapi</id>
      <activation>
        <property><name>!native</name></property>
      </activation>
      <dependencies>
        <dependency>
          <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
          <artifactId>quarkus-smallrye-openapi</artifactId>
        </dependency>
      </dependencies>
    </profile>
  </profiles>
</project>
